From f9aef9cfe61b97007f2a06fcc1c5d5bf09b4028c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:59:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore: patterns, conic gradients and stops of any alpha
 through OpenGPU

GraphicsContextOpenGPU now draws three things it used to hand to cairo
(cairo-out item 2, openamigabrowser docs/STEP5-6-WEBCORE.md):

- Pictures as paint (OpenGPUPaintAmiga's struct obgpu_pattern,
  obgpu_fill_path_pattern and obgpu_stroke_path_pattern: PATH_FILL and
  PATH_STROKE with OGPU_PATH_PATTERN). The picture is in a slot and its
  tile is repeated under its matrix times the transform.
  - drawPattern follows CairoUtilities' drawPatternToCairoContext: the
    tile under the pattern transform from the phase (reduced by whole
    tiles, as cairo's), filled over the destination rectangle by the
    options' operator.
  - Fills and strokes with a pattern brush follow PatternCairo: the tile
    image under the pattern space, repeated.
  - Limits: tiles on whole pixels, no spacing, normal blending. Filters
    as cairo's: FAST nearest and BILINEAR; GOOD or BEST only where the
    picture isn't shrunk or turned. Brushes must repeat both ways and
    have no global alpha.
  - Nearest sampling breaks ties as pixman does: a centre exactly between
    two pixels takes the one before.
- Conic gradients (OBGPU_CONIC, stream.h's OGPU_GRAD_CONIC). WebCore's
  angle starts at the top and OpenGPU's at the x axis, so the angle loses
  a quarter turn. cairo drew them as a mesh of Bezier sectors.
- Gradient stops of different alphas are drawn. OpenGPU mixes them
  premultiplied, as CSS says; pixman mixed them unpremultiplied. Dale
  accepted this on 1 November 2026.

gfopengpu.library: OBGPU_CAN_ALIASED is now set from gfopengpu.library 0.24's
OGPU_QUERY_FLAG(OGPU_OP_PATH_FILL, OGPU_PATH_ALIASED_BIT), on ARGB32 and
A8, instead of the library's revision. An older library, or a driver that
doesn't take the flag, answers OGPU_NONE.
